"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][21]={"university_biology_en:M21-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M21-L1 – NGS and experimental design<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Genomics and Systems Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L6<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>: sequence data, quality\nscores and experimental design.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare sequencing strategies\nagainst a research question, calculate nominal coverage and distinguish\ntechnical depth from biological replication.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Genomic research begins not with an instrument choice but with a\ndecision about what information must be obtained from DNA or RNA.\nDetecting small variants, reconstructing chromosome structure and\nquantifying transcripts place different demands on data and models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"sequencing-strategies-and-information-content\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sequencing\nstrategies and information content<\/h3>\n<p>Short reads provide many relatively short sequence segments. When\nmapping is unambiguous they can support small-variant analysis, whereas\nrepetitive regions complicate assignment. Long reads span larger\ncontinuous regions and help investigate structural differences,\nrepetitive regions and linked variants. Error models, costs and\navailable data volumes depend on the platform. Paired-end data contain\ntwo reads per fragment; the two ends are not independent biological\nsamples. Reference-based analyses can disadvantage sequences that differ\nstrongly from the reference. De novo assembly reconstructs sequence\nrelationships without a prescribed complete reference, but remains\ndependent on factors such as repeat length and coverage.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindividual reads with mean length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a target sequence of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnominal coverage is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>G<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\frac{NL}{G}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>When counting read pairs, bases at both ends must be included. This\ncalculation describes sequenced bases per target base, not actual\ncoverage at every position. With idealised independent, uniformly\ndistributed starts, the number of reads at a position is approximately\nPoisson-distributed; the fraction lacking coverage is then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(D=0)\\approx e^{-C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRepeats, duplicates, overlaps, filtering and uneven sequencing violate\nmodel assumptions.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"experimental-design-and-quality-assessment\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Experimental design\nand quality assessment<\/h3>\n<p>Define the independent biological unit and planned comparison before\nanalysis. For group-level questions, more reads from the same individual\nmay improve its measurement but cannot replace additional individuals.\nBalancing groups across processing batches reduces confounding of\ntreatment and batch. Greater depth may help with rare features;\nvariation among independent units must also be considered. Quality\nreports document base quality, mapping rates, coverage distribution and\nmissing positions separately. RNA-seq is not expected to cover the whole\ngenome uniformly because gene expression varies greatly. Nominal genome\ncoverage is therefore not a measure of transcriptome completeness.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_110_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponential curve of the expected uncovered fraction under the idealised Poisson model.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Exponential curve of the expected\nuncovered fraction under the idealised Poisson model.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Exponential curve of the expected uncovered fraction\nunder the idealised Poisson model.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional plant target genome is 200 million bases long. Forty\nmillion individual reads of 150 bases yield six billion sequenced bases\nand hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf only 80% of bases are counted as usable mapped bases, their average\ncoverage is 24. Under idealised nominal coverage of 3, a fraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0498<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-3}\\approx0.0498<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains uncovered. At 30 the ideal fraction is almost zero; real gaps\ncan nevertheless persist. Two groups of four plants constitute eight\nbiological units even if multiple data files exist per plant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate nominal coverage for 20 million read pairs, each\ncontaining two 100-base reads, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmillion bases.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a high mean coverage conceal problematic gaps?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A treatment was measured entirely in batch A and the control\nentirely in batch B. Why is post hoc batch correction alone not a\nsolution?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=20\\cdot10^6\\cdot2\\cdot100/10^8=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Highly covered regions can raise the mean while repetitive or\ndifficult positions remain missing. Coverage distribution and fraction\nof covered positions must be assessed separately.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The two influences are completely confounded. Without extra\ninformation or an improved design their separate contributions are not\nidentifiable.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Numbers are teaching model values, not recommendations for actual\nsequencing projects. Poisson coverage assumes highly simplified random\nsampling. High nominal coverage guarantees neither correct variant calls\nnor representative sampling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The question determines the sequence information required; design\ndetermines the scope of inference. The next lesson compares RNA and\nprotein measurements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/genome-bioinformatics-2026/\">EMBL-EBI\n– Genome bioinformatics: from short- to long-read sequencing (2026)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Course overview: sequencing technologies and downstream analysis;\nindexed page · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/introduction-rna-seq-and-functional-interpretation-2026/\">EMBL-EBI\n– Introduction to RNA-seq and functional interpretation (2026)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nExperimental design and bulk RNA-seq workflows; indexed page · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M21-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M21-L2 – Transcriptomics and proteomics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Genomics and Systems Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L4<\/a>: proteins,\ntranscription and omics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret RNA and protein\nabundance as different measurements, calculate relative changes after\nsimple normalisation and distinguish error control at different\nlevels.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A gene can produce more RNA without a proportional increase in\nprotein abundance. Translation, protein degradation and time delays\ninfluence the relationship between the layers. Joint biological\ninterpretation thus requires separate measurement models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"transcriptomics-counts-and-statistical-models\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transcriptomics:\ncounts and statistical models<\/h3>\n<p>Bulk RNA-seq measures sequence fragments from a cell population.\nAssignment to genes or isoforms depends on annotation and sequence\nuniqueness. Library size is one influence on the resulting raw counts.\nCounts per million (CPM) divide a count by the total number of counted\nfragments and multiply by one million. CPM is an intuitive relative\nmeasure, but when RNA composition changes greatly, a constant absolute\nabundance can have a changed relative value. Approaches such as DESeq2\nestimate size factors more robustly under their own assumptions and\nmodel counts with a negative binomial distribution. Biological\nreplicates support dispersion estimation; multiple tests require error\ncontrol, such as control of the false discovery rate (FDR).<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"proteomics-peptides-and-protein-inference\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Proteomics: peptides\nand protein inference<\/h3>\n<p>Mass spectrometry-based proteomics often investigates peptides from\nwhich proteins are inferred. One peptide can match several proteins.\nPeptide-spectrum matches must therefore be distinguished from peptide\nand protein levels. Identification error control can use target and\ndecoy databases; identification FDR is not automatically FDR for\ndifferential protein abundance. Quantitative signals depend on\nmeasurement platform, peptide characteristics and normalisation. A\nprotein that is not detected may be below the detection limit or absent\nfor technical reasons. Missing values are not automatically zero.\nRepositories such as PRIDE provide mass spectrometry data and evidence\nfor identifications.<\/p>\n<p>For comparable positive normalised values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlog-base-two fold change is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(B/A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDifferences between such values across omics layers, however, do not\ndescribe translation rates without additional assumptions. Time-matched\nmeasurements, appropriate referencing and independent replicates are\ncrucial for further inference.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_111_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two bars show different log fold changes in RNA and protein measurements.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two bars show different log fold changes\nin RNA and protein measurements.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two bars show different log fold changes in RNA and\nprotein measurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In a fictional pair of RNA libraries, one gene has 100 and 300\nassigned fragments out of one and two million counted fragments\nrespectively. The values are 100 and 150 CPM. Their ratio is 1.5 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.585<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(1.5)\\approx0.585<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhereas the raw count ratio is three. A separate protein measurement\ngives 80 and 100 normalised intensity units: ratio 1.25,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.322<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(1.25)\\approx0.322<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth changes are positive but not equal. Without biological replicates\nthe pair does not support a reliable significance test.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate CPM and log-base-two fold change for 50 out of 500,000 and\n200 out of 1,000,000 fragments.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a peptide shared by two proteins not unambiguous evidence for\neach individual protein?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must a 1% identification FDR not be interpreted directly as the\nprobability of error for a particular expression comparison?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">100 and 200 CPM;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(2)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The sequence information does not distinguish the two possible\norigins. Further unique evidence or protein grouping is required.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Identification FDR concerns a set of accepted identifications and\nits expected false proportion. Differential analysis tests different\nhypotheses with its own uncertainty and multiple-testing\ncorrection.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All measurements are simulated. CPM illustrates normalisation but\ndoes not replace a complete differential expression model. RNA and\nprotein intensities have different scales and are not directly\ncomparable as molecule counts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Multiple omics layers provide complementary evidence. Systems biology\nmodels next connect such evidence with hypothetical mechanisms.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/introduction-rna-seq-and-functional-interpretation-2026/\">EMBL-EBI\n– Introduction to RNA-seq and functional interpretation (2026)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nExperimental design and bulk RNA-seq workflows; indexed page · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/materials/proteomics-bioinformatics-materials/\">EMBL-EBI\n– Proteomics bioinformatics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mass spectrometry, identification,\nquantification and repositories; indexed page · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-014-0550-8\">Love, Huber and\nAnders (2014), Moderated estimation of fold change and dispersion for\nRNA-seq data with DESeq2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Count models, size factors and\ndispersion; indexed article text · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/online/courses/proteomics-an-introduction/proteomics-resources-at-the-ebi/pride/\">EMBL-EBI\n– PRIDE<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mass spectrometry repository, peptide and protein\nidentifications; indexed page · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M21-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M21-L3 – Networks and mathematical models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Genomics and Systems Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>: dynamics, kinetics and\ndifferential equations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Represent biological networks as\ndirected graphs and dynamic models, determine steady states and time\nconstants, and distinguish identifiability from mere fit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A network diagram displays possible interactions. It does not\nautomatically explain how quickly a system responds, how large a change\nwill be or whether a state is stable. Systems biology supplements\ntopology with quantitative relationships and tests them against\ndata.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"graphs-reaction-balances-and-models\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Graphs, reaction balances\nand models<\/h3>\n<p>Nodes may represent genes, proteins, metabolites or biological\nstates. Directed edges indicate an assumed direction of influence;\ncorrelations, by contrast, are often undirected relationships. A highly\nconnected node is not necessarily a causal regulator. For a reaction\nnetwork with concentration vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nstoichiometric matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand reaction rate vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(x,\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na deterministic balance can be written as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dx}{dt}=S v(x,\\theta).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A steady state satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Sv=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nindividual fluxes may remain nonzero. In an open system a constant input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be balanced by linear loss\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor two sequential pools\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x,y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the same concentration units, let<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=u-kx,\\qquad\\dot y=kx-hy.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k,h&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare inverse times and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis concentration per time. The steady state is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^*=u/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^*=u/h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe eigenvalues of the linear system matrix are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nboth are negative, so the steady state is asymptotically stable. The\nmodel assumes linear rates, homogeneous compartments and constant input.\nNonlinear saturation or feedback can change the dynamics.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"model-testing-and-exchange\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model testing and exchange<\/h3>\n<p>Parameters can be estimated from time series and multiple input\nconditions. A steady-state value alone identifies only the ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the first equation, not both parameters independently. Local\nsensitivity describes how a model quantity responds to small parameter\nchanges; it is not identical to statistical uncertainty. Alternative\nmodels can explain the same data comparably well. Independent\npredictions, residuals and uncertainty analysis are therefore more\nimportant than goodness of fit alone. SBML offers machine-readable model\nrepresentation; parameter values, units, initial conditions, simulation\nmethods and data provenance still need documentation. Constraint-based\nmodels use different assumptions, such as steady-state mass balances and\nflux bounds, and do not automatically provide time courses.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_112_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two coupled pools and two rising curves with the same steady value but different time constants.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two coupled pools and two rising curves\nwith the same steady value but different time constants.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two coupled pools and two rising curves with the same\nsteady value but different time constants.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>For fictional pools, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=6\\,\\mu\\mathrm{M/min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.5\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.25\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSteady concentrations are 12 and 24 micromolar. Characteristic times\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/k=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/h=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nminutes differ. At steady state input and output are both 6 micromolar\nper minute. When the constant input is turned on from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=12(1-e^{-0.5t})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nafter two minutes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis about 7.59 micromolar. A steady measurement of 12 alone would also be\ncompatible with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut the time course would be slower.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the same units.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Sv=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nExplain using the open model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What additional information helps distinguish the two alternative\nparameter pairs for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^*=20\\,\\mu\\mathrm{M}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^*=10\\,\\mu\\mathrm{M}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Flux through both pools at constant concentrations equals the\npositive input; inflows and outflows cancel in the balance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A time-resolved rise after a known input or an independent flux\nmeasurement can constrain the parameters better.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All parameters are hypothetical. Stability follows from this linear\nmodel, not a universal property of biological networks. A\nmachine-readable model is not yet an experimentally validated model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Models make assumptions about biological relationships explicit. The\nnext lesson examines joint analysis of multiple high-dimensional\nmeasurements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://sbml.org/documents/what-is-sbml/\">SBML – What is\nSBML?<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Exchange of computational models and model annotation;\nindexed page · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M21-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M21-L4 – Multidimensional data interpretation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Genomics and Systems Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L2<\/a>: dispersion,\nvisualisation and omics measurement models.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Integrate multidimensional\nmeasurement matrices by sample identity and scaling, interpret principal\ncomponents and distinguish exploratory patterns from validated\npredictions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>When the same biological samples are investigated at RNA, protein and\nmetabolite levels, different data matrices result. Joint analysis aims\nto expose shared and layer-specific variation without misrepresenting\nmeasurement differences as biological effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"data-matching-and-dimensionality-reduction\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data matching and\ndimensionality reduction<\/h3>\n<p>Matrix rows may represent samples and columns measured features.\nBefore joint analysis, samples are matched using unique identifiers.\nJoining by row position would be wrong if the ordering differs. Missing\nassays, different time points and ambiguous mappings must be documented.\nSuitable transformations depend on data type; relative intensities,\ncounts and concentrations are not equivalent.<\/p>\n<p>Principal component analysis (PCA) projects centred data onto\northogonal directions of maximal variance. When features are\nstandardised to mean zero and standard deviation one, the covariance\nmatrix is the correlation matrix. For two standardised features with\ncorrelation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>r<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>r<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;r\\\\r&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndirections\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)/\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-1)/\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave eigenvalues\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first component explains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+r)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof total variance. Eigenvector signs are arbitrary. A component\nrepresents a statistical pattern, not necessarily a biological process.\nIn joint PCA of many features, a feature-rich assay can dominate even if\nevery feature is individually standardised. Methods such as MOFA model\nshared and view-specific latent factors; their interpretation requires\nsubject-specific and statistical validation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"confounding-and-external-assessment\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Confounding and external\nassessment<\/h3>\n<p>PCA plots coloured by group, processing batch and other metadata aid\nexploration. Apparent separation by treatment may also arise from batch,\nage or sample quality. If a confounder is completely associated with the\ntarget variable, it cannot simply be removed. In prediction models,\nstandardisation, feature selection and model optimisation must not use\ninformation from the subsequent test data. Repeated measurements from\none person or organism must be considered when splitting data. An\nindependent test set estimates generalisability better than fit to\ntraining data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_113_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two orthogonal eigendirections explaining 90 and 10 percent of variance.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two orthogonal eigendirections explaining\n90 and 10 percent of variance.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two orthogonal eigendirections explaining 90 and 10\npercent of variance.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Two standardised fictional features have correlation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEigenvalues are 1.8 and 0.2; the first component explains 90% of total\nvariance. A sample with standardised values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.414<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}\\approx1.414<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the chosen first direction and zero on the second. A sample\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas first score zero and second score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first component thus captures shared variation, the second\ndifferences between features. Whether shared variation reflects a\nbiological state or a technical disturbance must be investigated\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndetermine the eigenvalues and proportion of variance explained by the\nfirst component.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why would matching two omics tables by row position be risky?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must standardisation for prediction be estimated within training\ndata?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">1.4 and 0.6; proportion\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.4/2=0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor 70%.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Tables may be sorted differently or samples may be missing.\nIncorrectly paired data generate misleading relationships; unique sample\nidentifiers are necessary.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Otherwise means and variation from the test set influence model\nconstruction. Evaluation is no longer fully independent.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The two-dimensional matrix is a teaching example. Real data may\ncontain nonlinear, compositional, missing or time-dependent\nmeasurements. High explained variance means neither high biological\nrelevance nor causal evidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Multidimensional analyses require documented data matching, model\nassumptions and independent evaluation. The next lesson links these\nsteps in a traceable workflow.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/materials/systems-biology-large-datasets-biological-insight-materials/integration-using-mofa-2/\">EMBL-EBI\n– Multi-omics data integration using MOFA<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Unsupervised factor\nanalysis for multi-omics data; indexed page · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/BatchQC.html\">Bioconductor\n– BatchQC<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Batch assessment, differential expression and PCA;\nindexed page · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M21-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M21-L5 – Reproducible pipelines<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Genomics and Systems Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L6<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L5<\/a>: data processing and\nscientific documentation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Plan a reproducible pipeline as\na dependency graph, preserve traceable inputs and versions, and\ndistinguish technical reproducibility from biological validation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A genomic analysis may involve many files and programs. Without\ndocumented inputs, references, parameters and software versions, it is\ndifficult to identify why two results differ. A workflow makes\ndependencies between processing steps explicit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"dependencies-provenance-and-failure-behaviour\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dependencies,\nprovenance and failure behaviour<\/h3>\n<p>A pipeline can be represented as a directed acyclic graph (DAG).\nNodes are tasks; edges denote required inputs or prior results. Two\nindependent quality checks can run in parallel; a joint analysis\nrequires both. If a check fails, the output must not silently be treated\nas complete. A run record includes input files, checksums, parameters,\nreference data and version, software versions, return codes and output\npaths. Containers can package dependencies but guarantee neither\nbit-identical output on every hardware platform nor correct scientific\nassumptions. A fixed revision and immutable container digest are more\ninformative than a mutable tag such as <code>latest<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>Workflow systems such as Nextflow separate processing descriptions\nfrom execution. Caching may reuse matching intermediate results, but a\ncache hit does not replace checking data validity. A good workflow has\nexplicit success criteria, small test data, expected outputs and\ntraceable rules for missing values. The final report also documents\nfailed runs and deviations. With personal genomic data, reproducibility\nmust not be equated with public release; access and sharing are subject\nto applicable privacy and consent requirements.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"a-deterministic-model-test\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A deterministic model test<\/h3>\n<p>The following Python example checks fictional measurements with\nneutral sample identifiers. It is not a sequencing or laboratory\nprotocol. Inputs are serialised in a stable order and hashed; the\nsummary is checked against an expected result. The checksum detects\ndifferences in the chosen serialisation, not errors in measurement.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> hashlib<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> json<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> statistics <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> mean<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>records <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&quot;P3&quot;<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;P1&quot;<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;P2&quot;<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>serialized <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> json.dumps(records, sort_keys<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                        separators<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;,&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;:&quot;<\/span>)).encode(<span class=\"st\">&quot;utf-8&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> serialized <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">b&#39;{&quot;P1&quot;:2,&quot;P2&quot;:4,&quot;P3&quot;:6}&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>digest <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> hashlib.sha256(serialized).hexdigest()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>result <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> mean(records.values())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> result <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(records), result, <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(digest))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The output is <code>3 4 64<\/code>. Changing P3 to 9 changes the input\nhash and mean. The same content in a different insertion order produces\nthe same hash through sorted serialisation. The 64 hexadecimal\ncharacters represent 256 bits; length alone does not establish equality\nbetween hashes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_114_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two parallel quality checks feed joint processing and the report.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two parallel quality checks feed joint\nprocessing and the report.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two parallel quality checks feed joint processing and the\nreport.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In a fictional project, quality check A requires 4 minutes and B\nrequires 6 minutes. Merging takes 3 minutes after both checks finish,\nfollowed by a 2-minute report. With no resource bottlenecks or start\ndelays, the longest dependency path takes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max(4,6)+3+2=11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nminutes. Fully sequential execution would take 15 minutes. The\ndifference concerns computational organisation, not the strength of the\nbiological evidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What mean does the Python example produce if P3 changes to 9?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the shortest duration if B takes 8 minutes instead and all\nother durations stay the same.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What information besides a software container is required for\nanother team to trace the analysis?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2+4+9)/3=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max(4,8)+3+2=13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nminutes.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, exact inputs and reference versions, their checksums,\nparameters, pipeline revision, documented data processing and success\ncriteria, and appropriate access arrangements.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All measurements and run times are simulated. Parallel time is an\nidealised lower bound without startup overhead or resource constraints.\nThe Python test demonstrates only deterministic processing; the validity\nof a biological experiment must be assessed separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>This elective connects data acquisition, modelling and traceable\nanalysis. Further master’s elective profiles are alternative\nspecialisations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://training.nextflow.io/latest/nextflow_run/04_remote_repositories/\">Nextflow\ntraining – Run remote pipelines<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Pinning a pipeline revision;\nindexed page · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://training.nextflow.io/latest/nextflow_run/\">Nextflow\ntraining – Nextflow Run<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Workflow mechanics and containerised\ndependencies; indexed page · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
